{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>By this you shall know that I am YHWH.</b> You said, 'I do not know YHWH,' and you do not recognize Me — I shall act so that you recognize Me. And so it is written (Exodus 14:4): 'וידעו מצרים כי אני ה׳' — 'And the Egyptians shall know that I am YHWH.'" "<b>Further,</b> 'By this you shall know that I am YHWH.' By way of the plain sense (peshat): through this plague and those like it, it is fitting for you to know that I am YHWH. By way of the Kabbalah: 'By this you shall know that I am YHWH' — he explains that the unique, hidden Name becomes known through the attribute called זאת ('this'), and it is the great hand which YHWH wrought in Egypt, and through it they knew the Name in the end. And concerning Pharaoh it is mentioned, for the cruelty of his heart (Exodus 7:23): 'ולא שת לבו גם לזאת' — 'and he did not set his heart even to this.'" "<b>Behold, I strike with the staff that is in my hand.</b> Behold, I — by My decree — to teach you that the waters turned to blood in appearance, in taste, and in smell. In appearance, as it is said: 'ויך את המים אשר ביאור לעיני פרעה ולעיני עבדיו' — 'and he struck the waters that were in the river, in the sight of Pharaoh and in the sight of his servants,' for they saw it thus with their own eyes. In taste, as it is said: 'והדגה אשר ביאור מתה' — 'and the fish that were in the river died'; fish live by the coolness of the water, and once it turned to blood the fish drank from it — and their life is sustained only by cool waters — and once the cool turned warm, they died immediately. And in smell, as it is said (Exodus 7): 'ויבאש היאור' — 'and the river stank.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:50.917583253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}